hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to talking  about the Gram stain receiver the Gram  stain is a very commonly used method in  laboratory and medical settings it is  called a differential stain so that  means that it helps someone to  differentiate or distinguish between two  different classes of bacteria  specifically gram positive bacteria and  gram-negative bacteria for more  information on the differences between  those two types of bacteria see my video  on gram-positive and gram-negative  bacteria today we're going to talk about  the steps of the Gram stain it starts  with fixing a sample this is when you  have a biological sample that has been  heat fixed to the slide simply by  passing it over a flame this helps the  sample containing all of the bacteria to  stick to the slide through all of the  subsequent steps the second step is the  use of is the use of crystal violet dye  this is a purple dye that stains all of  the bacteria so you can see here that  after we use this crystal violet dye all  of the bacteria in the sample are going  to stain purple this is because crystal  violet dye associ  with peptidoglycan and all bacteria have  at least some peptidoglycan and their  cell walls now crystal violet is a  positively charged molecule and iodine  is a negatively charged molecule this  means that together they form a complex  iodine for this reason it's called the  mordant  it helps the crystal violet to link to  the peptidoglycan and gram-positive cell  walls because they have a much thicker  layer of peptidoglycan then these cells  after they've been treated with the  crystal violet primary stain and the  iodine mordant they are treated with a  decolorizing solution but the polarizing  solution is is it's usually some form of  alcohol that helps the crystal violet  and iodine to be washed away from cells  where they aren't very strongly adhering  these would be the gram-negative cell  walls that only have a very very thin  layer  peptidoglycan leaving the crystal violet  dye in the iodine and the gram-positive  cell walls which have that much thicker  layer of peptidoglycan so after using  this decolorizing solution you have the  gram positive cells still retaining that  purple crystal violet dye  and then you have gram-negative cells  that are suddenly colorless again so  that is from the decolorizing solution  at this point you can visualize which  cells are grande positives because they  have retained this crystal violet dye  out there using the colorizing solution  but then the final step is the use of a  counter stain saccharin which has a pink  color just to help distinguish further  between those two cell types so after  the use of safranin crystal violet is  still in all of the gram-positive cell  walls so that these are all still purple  stained and you can distinguish that  they aren't grande positive whereas the  gram-negative cell walls have taken up  this safranin counterstain making them  at the end of a procedure like this you  can see how it's fast it's inexpensive  it's relatively simple and one of the  reasons it's so useful in a laboratory  or hospital setting is it helps to give  what's called a presumptive diagnosis we  see that the majority of cells in the  sample our grand positive bronze so  purple gram positive rod-shaped bacteria  the most common are one of the most  common causes of hospital-acquired  diarrhea and adults  it's called Clostridium difficile and it  is a gram positive rod-shaped bacterium  it is the most likely cause of the  infection  those who the sample was taken from and  then knowing this information not only  do you have a presumptive diagnosis that  can then be confirmed through later  studies we also know what kind of  bacteria were working with which helps  doctors to prescribe the correct  antibiotic regimen to treat this  infection and so this is why a Gram  stain  works
 

